	In the endgame, I prefer to carry two bags of holding, with my
gear (particularly uncursing supplies) split between them. The potential
for catastrophic typos isn't any higher than with the wand of
cancellation in a sack solution (though that solution does give you a
tiny chance that the insulating layer will save you from the
consequences of your mistake), and there are a number of advantages to
it.

	The extra weight of the second bag is not an issue. It's
negligible, for starters, but moreover, using two bags of holding means
that you can skip the whole sack with cancellation and holy water thing,
thus saving more encumbrance than the second bag uses, because it's
extremely unlikely that both bags will become cursed at the same time,
and in any other scenario, you can use the supplies from one bag to
uncurse/bless the other.

	Because each bag has half as much stuff in it, if one bag does
become unblessed or cursed, the encumbrance hit from it is much less,
making it more an annoyance to be dealt with at the first convenient
opportunity than an emergency that must be dealt with immediately. For
the same reason, they're not totally unmanagable when they become
cursed, so you can use regular uncursing methods like casting remove
curse or dipping in holy water instead of having to drop them and cancel
them where they lie.

	And if one bag *does* get destroyed, you've still got half your
stuff. (Assuming, of course, that you didn't destroy it with the other
bag.)

> Or do you just split your loot between one BoH in a stash and one on
> your person? That seems workable, except that really I want all of my
> wands of death and teleport with me, especially in the endgame.

	I don't use bags of holding for stashes, if there's any other
container available, because there's no advantage to it, and it would
expose my stash to potential destruction. Also, doing so would mean that
there were certain items I couldn't put in my stash. One of those in
particular, bags of tricks, I generally don't want to carry, don't want
lying around where monsters can get to them, and don't always want to
immediately empty.

